Vendored third-party code
=========================

avbtool.py
----------

Upstream: Android Open Source Project, `external/avb`
URL:      https://android.googlesource.com/platform/external/avb/+/refs/heads/main/avbtool.py
          (base64 via `?format=TEXT`)
Fetched:  2026-09-30, branch `refs/heads/main`
Version:  avbtool 1.3.0 (AVB_VERSION_MAJOR.MINOR.SUB in the file)
License:  Apache License 2.0 (copyright header preserved verbatim)

Local modifications
-------------------

Exactly one change: a 4-line comment block is inserted between the shebang and
the copyright header. It carries a `# ruff: noqa` directive so the file is not
linted or reformatted, plus a "do not edit" notice. Nothing else is touched --
every byte from the copyright header onwards is identical to upstream.

Two hashes, because there are two files:

  upstream, as fetched
      e5a664a38db623da00f080219bc0ee60a640a9dc4a872803616fae4938ac749b
  this file, as committed
      see VERIFY below (differs only by the header comment)

To re-verify, compare the committed file against upstream after stripping the
inserted comment block. It must match exactly:

    python - <<'EOF'
    import base64, hashlib, pathlib, urllib.request
    url = ("https://android.googlesource.com/platform/external/avb/+"
           "/refs/heads/main/avbtool.py?format=TEXT")
    upstream = base64.b64decode(urllib.request.urlopen(url).read())
    local = pathlib.Path("src/aik_py/_vendor/avbtool.py").read_bytes()
    # Everything from the copyright header onwards must be byte-identical.
    marker = b"# Copyright 2016, The Android Open Source Project"
    assert local[local.index(marker):] == upstream[upstream.index(marker):]
    print("OK: vendored file matches upstream after the license header")
    print("local sha256:", hashlib.sha256(local).hexdigest())
    EOF


Why vendored
------------

`avbtool` is not distributable on PyPI. There is no `avbtool` / `avb-tool` /
`avbpy` / `android-avb` package. The similarly named `pyavb` on PyPI is the
Avid video codec container format and is entirely unrelated to Android
Verified Boot.

The Bash toolkit this project replaces shipped AVB v1 signing only, via the
prebuilt `bin/boot_signer.jar`. That produces a `02 01 01 30 82` signature
block which is not an AVB v2 vbmeta, so images repacked by AIK are not
genuinely AVB-signed. Vendoring upstream avbtool gives real AVB v2
(`AVBf` footer + `AVB0` vbmeta) signing, which is the behaviour the Bash
toolkit lacked.
